<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">USS Charleston (C-2)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">USS Charleston was a protected cruiser of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a>, commissioned in 1889. In June 1898, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, she entered Apra Harbour on Guam and fired on the old fort; the Spanish governor, unaware that war had been declared, surrendered the island without a fight. She was wrecked off the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1899.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uss-charleston-c-2.jpg" alt="USS Charleston (C-2)" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USS_CHARLESTON_(C-2).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Lacking experience in building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> cruisers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> bought the design from the British firm Armstrong, Mitchell and Co. of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a>, whose plans resembled the Armstrong-built Naniwa, and had the ship built by Union Iron Works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>. Her propulsion machinery proved troublesome, apparently combining parts of several plants, and the yard had to make costly changes. She was launched on 19 July 1888 and commissioned on 26 December 1889 under Captain George C. Remey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The fourth American protected cruiser, she succeeded the Atlanta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and Chicago with better protection and higher speed. She carried two 8-inch guns in bow and stern barbettes and six 6-inch guns along her sides, though until a refit in 1891 four more 6-inch guns stood in for the 8-inch ones. Six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-fired boilers fed two horizontal compound engines of 7,500 horsepower, giving 18.2 knots on trials. She was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> ship with compound engines and carried no sails.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Leaving Mare Island on 10 April 1890 as flagship of the Pacific Squadron, she carried the remains of King David Kalakaua of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> and in 1891 joined the search for the Chilean steamer Itata during the Chilean Civil War. She took part in the International Naval Review at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 26 April 1893, patrolled off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> during the Brazilian Revolution and served in the Far East until she was put out of commission in July 1896.</p><p>Recommissioned on 5 May 1898 under Captain Henry Glass, she escorted troop transports from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> to Guam, arriving on 20 June. When she fired a challenge at Fort Santa Cruz, Spanish officials came out to apologise for having no powder to return the salute, and the next day a landing party received the surrender of the governor and the garrison of 69.</p><p>She joined Admiral George Dewey's fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> on 30 June, took part in the bombardment of 13 August and the capture of Subic Bay in September 1899, and on 2 November 1899 ran aground on Guinápac Rocks near Camiguin Island, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>-hulled ship the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> lost. The gunboat Helena rescued her crew on 12 November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “USS Charleston (C-2)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1898, during the Spanish-American War, the cruiser <b>USS Charleston</b> captured the island, and Spain ceded it to the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/guam">Guam</a></li></ul></section>
